00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:17.089
What did our plan look like?

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:22.060
How did we go through some
process and culture shifts,

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.130
some of the lessons learned.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:27.610
And also a little bit about
what we are working on today.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:32.510
So this is where we started.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:36.400
So if you were to look
at the image here,

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:45.166
this was our first ever
home page in November 1996.

00:04:45.166 --> 00:04:47.290
And if you can read the
line at the bottom it says,

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:48.831
"please open your
window to the width

00:04:48.831 --> 00:04:51.280
of the line of this text."

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.520
So clearly, not a
responsive design.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:58.740
It was mostly a GIF
with an image map,

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.360
where you could click
through into stories,

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.040
and we've come a
long way from that,

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:05.560
as you saw in the video clip.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:11.930
Today, the website, nytimes.com,
the main web site, runs on GKE.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:15.580
It's a managed Kubernetes
platform by Google.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:18.970
There have been a lot of talks,
including the keynote about it.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:20.920
We embraced a lot
of Cloud-native ways

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:26.380
of deploying and building stuff
that allowed us to move faster.

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:29.020
And we shut down our data
centers this year in April,

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:32.200
so we don't have co-located
data centers where

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:33.970
we run our website out of.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:38.480
And this is what it
looks like today.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:41.020
The home page, you
don't need to open it

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:42.970
to the width of the
line of some text.

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:44.650
We are responsive.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:47.140
It will resize.

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:53.710
But the homepage is a fairly
complex mishmash of content.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:59.260
And also, we have our article
pages, our interactive,

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:00.040
and graphics.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:02.800
That one, we did for
the 2016 election.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.110
So for special events, we do
special apps to tell stories

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:10.880
in a specific way.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:13.910
There's newer products that
we've built along the way,

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:14.840
like our cooking app.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:18.400
We've always had
recipes in the newspaper

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.407
that we brought together,
and like, you know,

00:06:20.407 --> 00:06:22.240
built a cooking app,
where people can share,

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.980
annotate recipes,
and things like that.

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:28.220
The crosswords app
has a life of its own.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:32.740
It is our highest
grossing product,

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:36.320
and it's a very
popular one as well.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:39.100
So all the things
that I'm talking about

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:42.010
require a lot of things
behind the scenes.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:43.360
We're talking about APIs.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.810
We're talking about APIs that
manage our content, our content

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:51.280
management systems that the
newsroom uses to actually write

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.600
and build these experiences,
the consumer revenue

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:57.760
side of things, where
we manage subscriptions

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:04.750
and make sure that we are
also reporting on those.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:10.510
Our archives are a great source
of research for institutions,

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:14.380
and students, and professors,
and researchers alike,

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:18.139
and then lastly, our mobile
platforms, our mobile apps.

00:07:18.139 --> 00:07:19.930
The way people consume
content is changing,

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:24.130
so mobile apps are
a ubiquity for us.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:25.840
But there were a
lot of challenges

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:30.250
to get from where we
were to where we are.

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:35.300
As of two years ago, the
challenges listed here

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.750
are pretty much the
challenges that a lot

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:44.710
of technology organizations face
as the landscape is shifting.

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:48.670
The "New York Times"
had a website in 1996.

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:54.010
Surprisingly, it's been
in existence online longer

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:56.410
than Google has been.

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:58.780
So we had an IT team that
was actually just managing

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:00.520
that website, which
was a very simple GIF

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:03.080
that someone would
upload and an image

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:04.840
map that people
could click through

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:09.900
into a larger infrastructure
team, many, many product teams.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:13.850
Organic growth sprouted lots
of products and platforms.

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:17.030
Everyone was doing things
a little bit differently,

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:20.030
sometimes significantly
differently,

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:21.360
and that's difficult to scale.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:23.790
Especially, when people start
doing things differently,

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:26.690
it becomes very hard very
quickly to move faster.

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:30.320
Like, you tend to build silos,
and you tend to work in silos.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:32.419
And any time you have
to work across silos,

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:35.669
it becomes very, very
hard for you to move.

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:38.419
As a result, a lot
of these-- a lot

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:41.600
of the apps that we
were trying to push out

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:45.534
were basically a spaghetti or
a mishmash of architectures

00:08:45.534 --> 00:08:48.200
that people put together to work
around some of the constraints,

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:51.380
because the IT infrastructure
team was very small,

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:53.390
and it couldn't scale very fast.

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:57.350
And the big problem
we had is that we

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:59.550
had to move very, very fast.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:02.430
I mean, we wanted to move at
the speed at which Google,

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.910
Facebook, and our
other competitors

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:07.310
are able to move
at today, and that

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:09.840
was becoming harder and harder.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:13.440
So we decided-- and we talked
long and hard about this,

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:15.380
so what we decided
to do was form

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:18.650
a team that would
evangelize best practices

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:21.410
and also build common
tooling for engineers,

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:26.270
kind of like what Google does
for all their other clients.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:29.450
But we decided very early
on that this was not

00:09:29.450 --> 00:09:31.581
a team that would run your
infrastructure for you,

00:09:31.581 --> 00:09:33.080
so if you were a
web app-- like, you

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:36.300
couldn't lob a web app over the
wall to an infrastructure team

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.050
and say, oh please, run
it and scale it for us.

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:41.750
We would ask teams to do that,
but we would provide the teams

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:44.300
with the support and the
tools needed to do that.

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:46.910
And we would help teams build
the operational maturity

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:50.810
along the way, and we would
manage some common tooling

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:51.800
along the way.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:55.240
So like everyone needs to
build and deploy applications--

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:58.550
everyone was talking about
continuous integration,

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:00.580
continuous deployment--

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:02.690
you need something to do that.

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:05.990
You need something
to manage secrets.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:09.890
You need a common way
for you to build stuff.

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:12.470
You also need to build
maturity as a part of your site

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:16.830
reliability
engineering practice.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:20.870
So we would work with teams
to help them understand

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:26.270
SRE practices, build tooling,
monitoring, et cetera, and also

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:28.250
a lot of test automation
which means building

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:32.080
tools for testing and
integrating those tools

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:33.484
with your CI/CD pipeline.

00:10:33.484 --> 00:10:35.900
So that was sort of the mandate
around which this team was

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:39.380
formed and evolving over the
course of the last two years,

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:42.920
and I will let Prashanth
talk about how we actually

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:47.640
implemented some of the
changes that I just mentioned.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:49.300
PRASHANTH SANAGAVARAPU:
Thanks, Deep.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:53.500
So as Deep mentioned, there
were challenges we were seeing,

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.060
and we founded
Delivery Engineering

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:56.150
to solve that problem.

00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:59.320
Now I'm going to talk
about how we saw migration

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:03.460
as an opportunity to
solve those challenges.

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:06.310
And I'll walk you
through what services

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:09.760
we chose from Google
and the strategy

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:13.060
that we implemented
for Cloud migration.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:16.180
And also, we'll look
at our migration plans.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:29.980
Sorry, it took a while
for that to pick up.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:32.590
So what we want are
the things that we

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:35.650
saw from our challenges,
and what we chose

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:37.190
is what I'm going
to talk in detail.

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:46.270
What we needed was a highly
scalable and reliable

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:47.330
infrastructure.

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:49.210
Our scaling needs
are spiky at times.

00:11:49.210 --> 00:11:51.290
When we send out a
breaking news alert,

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:53.740
we have to scale up pretty
quick to serve that traffic,

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:58.540
and it could be like
an event-based scale-up

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:02.520
where the scaling needs were,
like, based off events too.

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:04.560
Like, if we were to--

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:12.250
if we are hosting an
event like elections,

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:17.150
or Olympics, maybe
any special event,

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:19.270
we had to scale up
our infrastructure.

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:21.730
For that, what we
needed is to build

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:25.460
apps that could rely on reliable
infrastructure like GCS,

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:28.060
BigQuery, Bigtable, and GKE.

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:31.127
They all have published
SLAs, and they

00:12:31.127 --> 00:12:32.210
have pretty much reliable.

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:34.600
So we chose to work
with these services that

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:35.980
are managed by Google.

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:41.030
And Deep mentioned that
we wanted to be fast.

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:44.790
The way we wanted to be
fast is by bringing autonomy

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:48.150
in managing environments
for developers.

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:50.250
Over the years of
experience, what we found

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:53.400
is developers are more
knowledgeable about managing

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:55.440
their application
in case of an outage

00:12:55.440 --> 00:13:00.110
or shipping that huge
configuration change that

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:02.500
could make a lot of impact.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:06.180
So what we chose was to
use GCP projects as a tool

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:13.050
to provide that autonomy
and give developers

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:15.240
the full access to
their own environments

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:19.034
and help them manage their own
infrastructures in production.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.520
At the time when we were
in our data centers,

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:29.990
we saw how containers could
help maybe for scaling or maybe

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:36.010
for making the parity between
your dev stage environments.

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:39.160
And we wanted to adopt
container technology

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:43.060
as a main-scale way
of deploying, building

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:46.660
and deploying applications
to production.

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:48.950
At the time, we evaluated with--

00:13:48.950 --> 00:13:51.940
we were evaluating container
orchestration services

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:55.600
like Marathon Mesos,
Nomad, and Kubernetes.

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:57.520
In the end, as a
company, we decided

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:00.240
to adopt Kubernetes as our
container orchestration

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:01.380
service.

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:05.380
But if we were to
build and manage

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:08.540
Kubernetes infrastructure
for the entire company, then

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:13.660
a developer engineering team,
is a small team in number,

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:18.190
we'd have been dealing
with just managing

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:21.350
the nodes and clusters, and
patching them, and whatnot.

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:25.350
So when Google came out with
managed Kubernetes service,

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:27.760
which is GKE, it was
quite easy for us

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:32.650
to double down on GCP and
GKE and rely on Google

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:35.290
to manage our
infrastructure, that is,

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:36.730
the Kubernetes infrastructure.

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:40.540
So what we were looking at was
a true engineering partner,

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:44.590
who could provide
services and support

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:48.670
for building applications
as well as even run

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:49.940
those applications for us.

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:57.730
So that's how we chose
our GKE services, what

00:14:57.730 --> 00:15:00.010
we wanted to work with,
but we had to build up

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:02.230
a strategy for migration.

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:06.550
The way we we saw our
strategy was to build--

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:08.680
it's not just like
lift and shift

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:12.820
but lean onto the shift more,
meaning shift the processes

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:14.230
that we were working with--

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:15.470
that is our culture--

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:18.470
and the tooling that
supports this migration.

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:20.890
So we wanted to shift
the processes, culture,

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:24.260
and tooling.

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:27.370
So shift in processes,
as I mentioned,

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:32.620
we gave GCP projects as a way
to provide development autonomy

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:35.120
in managing their
own infrastructure.

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:37.480
What that really
meant was teams would

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:41.290
know what they own
in their GCP projects

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:44.900
and their destiny of
those services too.

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:48.290
We moved away from this culture
of having an ops team that

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:52.270
would manage their applications
in production to developers

00:15:52.270 --> 00:15:54.610
owning their
services end to end,

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:56.380
so that's a culture
shift that we did.

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:06.850
And traditionally, we were
working off a ticket-based

00:16:06.850 --> 00:16:11.350
system, and we wanted to change
that by building automation

00:16:11.350 --> 00:16:16.390
that are common across multiple
teams and projects by just

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:20.000
using off-the-shelf tools
like GCloud and [? kubectl, ?]

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:22.320
or [? kube-control, ?] whatever.

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:24.020
And we wrote documentation--

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:27.160
we built a lot of centralized
documentation and step-by-step

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:31.520
guides to onboard
teams to the automation

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:36.220
that Developer Engineering
built and helping them

00:16:36.220 --> 00:16:37.700
manage their infrastructure.

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:41.080
So we basically moved away
from ticket-based traditional

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:45.160
infrastructure way
of handling tasks

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:47.920
that are common between
application deployments

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:52.960
to much more self-service model
of building automation that

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:54.296
solved these common tasks.

00:16:58.030 --> 00:17:01.150
So as we were
migrating, we also saw

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:04.119
that we were going
into a different land

00:17:04.119 --> 00:17:08.440
altogether from a
data center to GCP.

00:17:08.440 --> 00:17:11.430
Not every team was aware
of how Kubernetes worked

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:14.119
or what GCP services could do.

00:17:14.119 --> 00:17:15.660
So we had to build
a lot of training.

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:20.109
We engaged with Google and
educated a lot of teams

00:17:20.109 --> 00:17:24.859
around how Kubernetes GKE could
be employed to build and apply

00:17:24.859 --> 00:17:27.410
their applications and so on.

00:17:27.410 --> 00:17:31.630
So what we did here is built
those transferable skills.

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:34.422
The culture shift is yes,
there is projects there.

00:17:34.422 --> 00:17:36.130
There are applications
that are building,

00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:38.710
but we needed to build
that consistency in the way

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:43.150
how these teams could work
with those environments.

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:45.340
The way how we did that
with that was with training.

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:48.050
We built those transferable
skills on teams,

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:52.030
so if an engineer were to move
from one team to another team,

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:54.775
they would be comfortable in
understanding that environment.

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:06.130
The culture shift in the way how
we managed our CI/CD processes.

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:09.820
So I gave a little bit of
an insight into everybody

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:12.400
gets a project, and
they're off their own,

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:14.350
and they can configure
whatever they want.

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:17.660
But the way how we wanted
to bring consistency

00:18:17.660 --> 00:18:22.160
in managing these environments
is by building automation

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:23.440
around CI/CD.

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:27.340
So we adopted Drone, a
container-based deployment

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:32.790
tool, to help
deploy applications.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:37.810
The shift that came out of
is we were not setting up

00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:41.950
build bots the a config
management tool with Jenkins,

00:18:41.950 --> 00:18:44.320
and teams don't have
to go back and forth.

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:48.700
All we have is just a YAML
file, where their pipeline is

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:52.360
described as code, and we, as
the Delivery Engineering team,

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:54.940
provided that YAML
file as a template.

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:59.230
So teams could extend that
template and deploy their app.

00:18:59.230 --> 00:19:03.400
So we made a shift from writing
configuration management

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:06.610
code for building app,
building build bots,

00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:10.180
and completely on container
technology with Drone.

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:12.580
So Drone and GitHub
are integrated,

00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:16.000
and if there is a
good push, then Drone

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:18.400
kicks in and deploys
to wherever it has to.

00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:24.770
Secrets, it's a big topic.

00:19:24.770 --> 00:19:27.440
When we are in our
data center, there

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:30.740
are various ways teams use
to manage their secrets.

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:34.850
It could be [? Gitlab, ?] or
it could be Puppet Hiera YAML.

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:37.400
So there were various ways,
and obviously that, too,

00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:40.020
[INAUDIBLE].

00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:43.690
We wanted to tackle
that by deploying Vault,

00:19:43.690 --> 00:19:49.250
HashiCorp Vault. Vault
is a single tool that

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:55.550
had been integrated into
our GitHub and Drone,

00:19:55.550 --> 00:20:02.000
so every team gets a secret
backend space for Vault. We

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:06.590
call it namespace, and teams
can write their secrets,

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:10.250
depending on their repositories
and push those secrets

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:16.310
into their namespace without
having to model with other team

00:20:16.310 --> 00:20:17.690
secrets.

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:23.870
So with Vault, today we have
about like 75 namespaces,

00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:27.110
and the way we
integrate it is if teams

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:29.900
have to build and
deploy new environments,

00:20:29.900 --> 00:20:32.720
Vault can completely take
care of these secrets

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:34.470
and deploy to
those environments.

00:20:34.470 --> 00:20:39.090
So we've adapted Vault, and
the policies of managing Vault

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:41.940
is also put into Git.

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:44.910
So we use policy management
as code with Vault now.

00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:56.460
When we were in
our data center, we

00:20:56.460 --> 00:21:01.830
used to have a physical
networking routing equipment

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:08.310
like NetScalers, and also we
had a monolith layer where we

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:12.180
used to create pull requests.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:14.520
And it was becoming
like a huge monolith

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:18.120
that was taking
our routing layer

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:21.690
into an unmanageable
state, so what

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:23.550
we did is we adapted Fastly.

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:29.340
We made that routing layer
into much more modularized

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:32.340
components that teams
can own and manage

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:38.820
their own routing routes in
terms of writing a VCL code.

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:43.710
So we moved away from
this ticket-based model

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:48.690
of changing routing
paths to modularizing

00:21:48.690 --> 00:21:56.090
and making it a code way of
handling the routing logic.

00:21:56.090 --> 00:22:00.330
Adopting Fastly,
we also achieved

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:03.930
I would say, in a much
more important feature

00:22:03.930 --> 00:22:08.460
is, what we did during the
migration is we wrote logic

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:13.860
to split the traffic
to applications in GCP

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:17.100
by a percentage basis like,
say, 5% of the traffic would

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:20.730
go to GCP applications,
understand how that traffic

00:22:20.730 --> 00:22:22.680
pattern would affect
that application,

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:25.920
and learn about these
services in GCP, and move on

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:29.810
and cut over to
maybe 50% or 70%,

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:33.390
and test out how the app
behaved with the modifications

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:36.120
that we were making to fit GCP.

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:38.760
And gradually, we cut
over from our data center

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:40.140
to GCP with Fastly.

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:52.270
Costs-- when we move from
a data center to Cloud,

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:53.920
cost is an important thing.

00:22:53.920 --> 00:22:57.370
For us, all of our operational
costs in the data center

00:22:57.370 --> 00:22:59.800
were budgeted in
a single budget,

00:22:59.800 --> 00:23:03.060
so teams really
didn't know or had

00:23:03.060 --> 00:23:06.810
to look into the operational
costs of each application.

00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:09.630
But when we gave
GCP projects, teams

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:13.060
had to have visibility into
how much resources that they're

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:17.670
using in the cloud, what we did
is Delivery Engineering, built

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:25.570
a budget bot that basically
alerts teams about the budgets

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:27.130
that they'll set
for the projects.

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:30.940
So people can set
budgets in a YAML file.

00:23:30.940 --> 00:23:32.770
As you can see here,
they're setting alerts

00:23:32.770 --> 00:23:36.580
for 10%, 20%, or 50%, or 75%.

00:23:36.580 --> 00:23:40.290
So the bot basically
sends a slack notification

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:44.690
when the GCP project hit
those budget thresholds,

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:47.350
so we just fortified our
made budgets into code.

00:23:50.890 --> 00:23:55.570
Overall, our shift in the way
how we use tooling and culture

00:23:55.570 --> 00:23:57.640
now looks like this.

00:23:57.640 --> 00:24:02.470
Most of our work is the adopt
space, GitHub based, your code,

00:24:02.470 --> 00:24:04.500
pipeline as code,
and infrastructure

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:06.730
as code with GKE and GAE.

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:09.970
And also, managing your secrets
and managing your policies

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:12.820
for secrets is also--

00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:17.110
now infrastructure as code
is tied into that notion.

00:24:17.110 --> 00:24:19.360
So basically, if
you're a developer,

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:21.280
all you do is get
[? comment, ?] push,

00:24:21.280 --> 00:24:25.690
and Drone does [? Kube ?]
Apply onto the GCP projects

00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:27.940
and pushes code into
the environments,

00:24:27.940 --> 00:24:30.930
so it's much more simpler and
consistent across the projects.

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:35.270
So now I talked
about the services

00:24:35.270 --> 00:24:38.270
we chose, the strategy
we used in the way of how

00:24:38.270 --> 00:24:41.620
we shifted our data center
applications into GCP.

00:24:41.620 --> 00:24:47.250
Now I'm going to talk about
how we executed our migration.

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:51.185
So when we were strategizing and
developing tools around a year

00:24:51.185 --> 00:24:53.300
and a half ago, the
"New York Times"

00:24:53.300 --> 00:24:58.460
decided to shut down our
data centers by Q1 of 2018.

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:00.170
And we didn't extend
our leases, so we

00:25:00.170 --> 00:25:01.520
had to meet those deadlines.

00:25:01.520 --> 00:25:04.940
That's the situation we were.

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:08.540
Basically, our planning was
on four major components

00:25:08.540 --> 00:25:13.820
that I will hit, which is
networking, data, application,

00:25:13.820 --> 00:25:16.100
and monitoring needs
that you are seeing.

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:20.360
So those are the four pillars
that I will cover as migration

00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:22.460
and transition plans.

00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:25.310
So coming from a
data center mindset,

00:25:25.310 --> 00:25:28.220
you all would have
had this experience.

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:32.450
Most of your apps need
physical network in a way

00:25:32.450 --> 00:25:34.410
how they connect
with each other.

00:25:34.410 --> 00:25:37.190
But as I mentioned, we
were going into a plane

00:25:37.190 --> 00:25:40.430
where all applications are
going to be in individual GCP

00:25:40.430 --> 00:25:45.530
projects, and we started trying
out fewer networking models

00:25:45.530 --> 00:25:48.460
to solve for the connectivity.

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:52.130
The main connections
that we needed

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:58.420
were our data center
to GCP project

00:25:58.420 --> 00:26:02.760
by a GCP project in a
hub-and-spoke model,

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:05.090
where there would be
a direct connection

00:26:05.090 --> 00:26:07.190
from a centralized
hub project where

00:26:07.190 --> 00:26:10.880
we would host our
infrastructure services like DNS

00:26:10.880 --> 00:26:15.590
and expand that with VPN
tunnels to other GCP projects.

00:26:15.590 --> 00:26:17.380
We experimented
with that, and then

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:20.320
we quickly realized that there
are limitations on how many VPN

00:26:20.320 --> 00:26:24.830
tunnels that you could connect
with on a single GCP project,

00:26:24.830 --> 00:26:28.730
and we couldn't move forward
with the hub and spoke model.

00:26:28.730 --> 00:26:31.970
And then we started looking
into VPN mesh networking

00:26:31.970 --> 00:26:35.150
to connect all the GCP
projects with each other

00:26:35.150 --> 00:26:37.880
whenever there is a connection
that's needed with VPN tunnels.

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:41.840
Even that didn't scale well,
because it was becoming

00:26:41.840 --> 00:26:44.540
a lot of overhead for our team
to maintain these connections

00:26:44.540 --> 00:26:47.490
and make sure the VPN tunnels
are healthy and whatnot.

00:26:47.490 --> 00:26:50.630
So finally, we met with Google.

00:26:50.630 --> 00:26:53.390
We went through our needs.

00:26:53.390 --> 00:26:57.530
What we needed was a way
to have our data center

00:26:57.530 --> 00:27:00.290
services exposed to
the services that

00:27:00.290 --> 00:27:03.950
are getting deployed
in GCP, so we achieved

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:07.370
that using the Cloud Endpoints.

00:27:07.370 --> 00:27:09.660
How many of you are
aware of Cloud Endpoints?

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:13.140
Have you experimented
with Cloud Endpoints?

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:15.620
That's quite a few.

00:27:15.620 --> 00:27:21.706
Cloud Endpoints is a proxy
that is built by Google.

00:27:21.706 --> 00:27:24.080
I would highly recommend--
there are not many people that

00:27:24.080 --> 00:27:25.374
raised their hands.

00:27:25.374 --> 00:27:26.790
I'm aware of the
Cloud Endpoints--

00:27:26.790 --> 00:27:29.960
I'll raise mine-- but
one thing I would say

00:27:29.960 --> 00:27:36.740
is this proved to be a
solution that we could use.

00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:40.130
I would highly recommend looking
at the way Cloud Endpoints can

00:27:40.130 --> 00:27:46.250
be deployed, but it's a
container, NGINX container,

00:27:46.250 --> 00:27:48.260
that Google provides.

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:50.430
And it has authentication.

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:52.590
It can help you
provide authentication

00:27:52.590 --> 00:27:56.400
against your services that
don't have auth right now.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:28:01.700
So we use Cloud Endpoints
to deploy our GCP projects

00:28:01.700 --> 00:28:03.530
to have connection between--

00:28:03.530 --> 00:28:06.980
prior to project on
a zero trust network.

00:28:06.980 --> 00:28:08.230
And that is Google's way.

00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:12.020
Google's prescribed way is
to implement TLS encryption

00:28:12.020 --> 00:28:15.020
everywhere and beyond
Zero Trust Network.

00:28:15.020 --> 00:28:17.560
We also deployed the
same Cloud Endpoints.

00:28:17.560 --> 00:28:21.175
Google cal;s it as
extended service proxy.

00:28:21.175 --> 00:28:23.750
We deployed that extended
service proxy in our data

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:27.350
centers to expose the services
that are in our data centers

00:28:27.350 --> 00:28:31.820
to the project and services that
are in multiple GCP projects.

00:28:31.820 --> 00:28:37.850
So that's how our network had
transformed from a physical VPN

00:28:37.850 --> 00:28:45.830
network to a zero trust TLS
encrypted-based network.

00:28:45.830 --> 00:28:48.270
And also, we started
moving away from firewalls.

00:28:48.270 --> 00:28:51.855
We rely on IAM roles to limit
access between services.

00:28:57.620 --> 00:29:01.870
Data-- not all of our
migrations were dump and load.

00:29:01.870 --> 00:29:05.780
What we did is we
started syncing data

00:29:05.780 --> 00:29:09.390
to GCS bucket and migrate
our data to services that

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:13.030
are managed by Google like
BigQuery, Bigtable, Datastore,

00:29:13.030 --> 00:29:15.020
Cloud SQL, and Spanner.

00:29:15.020 --> 00:29:16.900
It required a little
bit of rewriting,

00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:19.780
but we first migrated
our data, syncing them

00:29:19.780 --> 00:29:24.910
into these abstractions, and
we started cutting over slowly.

00:29:24.910 --> 00:29:28.210
So not all were dump and
load, but some of them were.

00:29:33.020 --> 00:29:37.660
So I mentioned our strategy was
to use GKE/GAE services that

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:40.160
are managed by Google.

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:43.690
And our planning, we made
sure that most of our apps

00:29:43.690 --> 00:29:45.910
have migrated to
those abstractions,

00:29:45.910 --> 00:29:49.340
then going into a
VM-based model where

00:29:49.340 --> 00:29:52.290
teams have to manage their VMs.

00:29:52.290 --> 00:29:55.420
This particularly helped
us in the migration,

00:29:55.420 --> 00:29:58.270
because teams were just
focusing on the features,

00:29:58.270 --> 00:30:00.910
and Google was managing their
infrastructure for them.

00:30:00.910 --> 00:30:04.000
So the transition was
much faster for us.

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:07.240
And what it did was, by
providing that consistent

00:30:07.240 --> 00:30:11.890
automation that Drone gave,
we converted all of our apps

00:30:11.890 --> 00:30:16.980
to fit into GKE/GAE model and
[? 12-factorizing ?] them.

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:19.270
Like I said, we
standardized our pipelines,

00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:23.650
and we used Google Deployment
Manager and Terraform to set up

00:30:23.650 --> 00:30:26.170
the basic infrastructure
that is needed

00:30:26.170 --> 00:30:27.580
before the deployments began.

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:36.870
How many of you here are
working with more than one

00:30:36.870 --> 00:30:38.120
monitoring logging tools here?

00:30:40.730 --> 00:30:42.380
Wow, That's nice.

00:30:42.380 --> 00:30:43.850
Cool.

00:30:43.850 --> 00:30:49.010
Usually, teams often ask for
that specific monitoring tool

00:30:49.010 --> 00:30:49.970
that they like.

00:30:49.970 --> 00:30:52.010
In the "New York
Times" we ended up

00:30:52.010 --> 00:30:54.530
having a slew of monitoring
and log-in tools.

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:58.400
As part of our migration, we
wanted to reduce that number.

00:30:58.400 --> 00:31:03.500
What we didn't have is a default
or a standard way of setting

00:31:03.500 --> 00:31:07.020
monitoring reports or
dashboards for alerts,

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:11.090
so what we started doing is
to rely on Google Stackdriver

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:13.530
for setting up that
basic monitoring

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:16.562
and alerting that
we had to provide.

00:31:16.562 --> 00:31:18.270
Today, we have slack
on [? page review ?]

00:31:18.270 --> 00:31:23.660
integrations with Stackdriver,
and every application

00:31:23.660 --> 00:31:26.690
has a basic dashboard
that we could--

00:31:26.690 --> 00:31:30.980
any team member could go and
look at and understand their

00:31:30.980 --> 00:31:35.460
[? SLIs. ?]

00:31:35.460 --> 00:31:39.470
So I talked about a lot
of strategy execution.

00:31:39.470 --> 00:31:41.760
That's all great,
but what we noticed

00:31:41.760 --> 00:31:46.900
is teams are at various levels
of operational maturity.

00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:48.750
So we had to up our
game over there,

00:31:48.750 --> 00:31:51.210
and I'll hand it
over to Deep to talk

00:31:51.210 --> 00:31:53.402
about operational maturity.

00:31:53.402 --> 00:31:57.250
DEEP KAPADIA: Is
something wrong with this?

00:31:57.250 --> 00:31:59.070
So yeah, like
Prashanth talked about,

00:31:59.070 --> 00:32:01.380
like the process of
the migration itself,

00:32:01.380 --> 00:32:05.970
but a great part of this was
building operational maturity

00:32:05.970 --> 00:32:10.350
into our practices and on
the teams that were actually

00:32:10.350 --> 00:32:13.660
going to build and maintain
these applications.

00:32:13.660 --> 00:32:18.210
So we realized very early on,
as Prashanth mentioned, not

00:32:18.210 --> 00:32:21.140
all development teams are at
the same level of maturity.

00:32:21.140 --> 00:32:23.130
Like, we had teams
that were absolutely

00:32:23.130 --> 00:32:25.830
thrilled at the prospect
of being in the cloud,

00:32:25.830 --> 00:32:29.600
and having all the autonomy,
and wanting to build everything

00:32:29.600 --> 00:32:31.350
from scratch versus
teams that just wanted

00:32:31.350 --> 00:32:33.720
to build their
little Node.js app

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:36.120
and just, like, have it
deployed somewhere in the cloud

00:32:36.120 --> 00:32:38.580
or just write
application features

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:41.830
but not really worry
about operations.

00:32:41.830 --> 00:32:44.310
So I think we had to level
set very early on with a lot

00:32:44.310 --> 00:32:48.030
of these teams, and we realized
that during the migrations

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:50.190
a lot of these teams
need a lot of support.

00:32:50.190 --> 00:32:54.359
So Prashanth mentioned
all the trainings

00:32:54.359 --> 00:32:56.400
that we did, all the
documentation that we wrote,

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:59.220
like, setting up the
right level of support

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:01.360
was very, very important.

00:33:01.360 --> 00:33:04.740
And one of the biggest things
we talked about with teams

00:33:04.740 --> 00:33:09.780
was being on call, like making
sure that we are giving teams

00:33:09.780 --> 00:33:13.710
a lot of autonomy to run their
infrastructure in the cloud,

00:33:13.710 --> 00:33:16.050
but at the same time it
comes with some level

00:33:16.050 --> 00:33:17.790
of responsibility.

00:33:17.790 --> 00:33:21.450
The level of responsibility
is around managing costs,

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:26.520
also managing their uptime
and their SLIs and SLOs

00:33:26.520 --> 00:33:28.290
on an ongoing basis.

00:33:28.290 --> 00:33:30.510
There was not going to be
a central infrastructure

00:33:30.510 --> 00:33:33.110
team or a centralized
system team going forward

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:37.110
to actually manage the
uptime for their apps

00:33:37.110 --> 00:33:40.750
and also be on call
for those apps.

00:33:40.750 --> 00:33:46.260
So one of the things we did was,
every time someone wanted to--

00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:48.930
someone decided that they were
going to move to the cloud,

00:33:48.930 --> 00:33:52.770
and they came up with a
plan, we would kick that

00:33:52.770 --> 00:33:55.890
off with application
readiness reviews just

00:33:55.890 --> 00:33:58.110
to assess where the
team and the application

00:33:58.110 --> 00:34:00.210
are from a maturity standpoint.

00:34:00.210 --> 00:34:02.860
It was very important
for us to do.

00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:05.760
So we started doing
it with certain teams,

00:34:05.760 --> 00:34:07.826
and then other teams just
jumped on it and said,

00:34:07.826 --> 00:34:09.659
oh, well, we also want
to move to the cloud.

00:34:09.659 --> 00:34:11.493
Can you help us
assess where we are,

00:34:11.493 --> 00:34:12.659
and what we need to work on?

00:34:12.659 --> 00:34:14.610
And like, the ideal
was to identify

00:34:14.610 --> 00:34:18.360
gaps, not just from a
technical standpoint but also

00:34:18.360 --> 00:34:20.400
for us to understand,
for our team

00:34:20.400 --> 00:34:23.940
to understand where we will
need to provide support,

00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:26.820
and how much support
will these teams need.

00:34:26.820 --> 00:34:29.310
We also instituted
regular office hours,

00:34:29.310 --> 00:34:31.800
where people could come
and talk to us about rando.

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:35.380
Questions, but at the same time
what the office hours did was

00:34:35.380 --> 00:34:39.030
it served as a community
building exercise of sorts.

00:34:39.030 --> 00:34:41.070
Like, people from
multiple teams would

00:34:41.070 --> 00:34:43.380
show up and talk
about their issues,

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:46.020
and then find solutions
to each others problems

00:34:46.020 --> 00:34:47.940
as a part of it,
which was excellent

00:34:47.940 --> 00:34:50.250
because that sort of took
us out of the picture,

00:34:50.250 --> 00:34:53.820
and the teams are actually
talking to each other

00:34:53.820 --> 00:34:55.170
and also--

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:56.909
sort of put our
engineers in front

00:34:56.909 --> 00:34:59.430
of the Google technical
account managers and,

00:34:59.430 --> 00:35:01.530
like, the Google
customer engineers that

00:35:01.530 --> 00:35:05.100
were embedded with us for
the course of the migration

00:35:05.100 --> 00:35:06.460
was really, really helpful.

00:35:06.460 --> 00:35:11.190
Thank you, Jim, [? Romey, ?]
[? Asher, ?] very much.

00:35:11.190 --> 00:35:14.850
And of course, you
know, they were there--

00:35:14.850 --> 00:35:18.280
it was a shared fate for both
of us, as well as Google.

00:35:18.280 --> 00:35:21.270
So they were there to support
us doing our major launches,

00:35:21.270 --> 00:35:24.180
during our major
migrations, et cetera.

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:27.240
And just before you
decide to switch over

00:35:27.240 --> 00:35:30.150
to production in the cloud,
let's do a production

00:35:30.150 --> 00:35:32.580
assessment and make sure
that you have all the things

00:35:32.580 --> 00:35:38.040
that you need, to make sure that
you are ready for a calamity

00:35:38.040 --> 00:35:41.850
or in case you have to fall back
to the data center for a while

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:43.080
what the plan for that is.

00:35:43.080 --> 00:35:45.630
So just doing a production
readiness review

00:35:45.630 --> 00:35:46.904
was very important.

00:35:49.690 --> 00:35:52.390
We read this book, right?

00:35:52.390 --> 00:35:55.730
Like, this is where most
of our ideas came from.

00:35:55.730 --> 00:35:58.860
I wish we had written
that book, but we didn't.

00:35:58.860 --> 00:36:04.220
But yeah, the idea was
evangelize reliability

00:36:04.220 --> 00:36:05.150
as a feature.

00:36:05.150 --> 00:36:07.760
To us, it was very important
that people understand

00:36:07.760 --> 00:36:11.080
that all the features that
you build into your product

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:13.360
are great, but at the
same time, if you do not

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:18.620
prioritize reliability of
your apps as a feature,

00:36:18.620 --> 00:36:20.120
it can all go to
waste very quickly.

00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:23.390
Like, it only takes
four spinning wheels

00:36:23.390 --> 00:36:25.960
on an app for me to
uninstall that app,

00:36:25.960 --> 00:36:28.300
and I'm guessing a
lot of other people

00:36:28.300 --> 00:36:33.490
are maybe as impatient as I
am or maybe a lot less patient

00:36:33.490 --> 00:36:34.840
than I am.

00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:38.410
So to me it's very important
that reliability is considered

00:36:38.410 --> 00:36:40.750
a feature, so it's not
something of an afterthought

00:36:40.750 --> 00:36:43.030
that you would think about.

00:36:43.030 --> 00:36:45.559
And that was a big
cultural shift,

00:36:45.559 --> 00:36:47.350
where teams started
thinking about building

00:36:47.350 --> 00:36:49.420
more and more reliability
into their apps,

00:36:49.420 --> 00:36:54.610
monitoring their SLIs, and
tracking their SLOs, et cetera,

00:36:54.610 --> 00:36:57.970
and sharing them
across the board.

00:37:01.090 --> 00:37:02.920
If a team knew that
there was a dependency

00:37:02.920 --> 00:37:05.080
for many other teams,
it was very important

00:37:05.080 --> 00:37:07.870
for them to build their SLOs
and share it with other teams.

00:37:07.870 --> 00:37:09.828
That's something that we
did through the course

00:37:09.828 --> 00:37:12.840
of the migration.

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:16.880
We worked with Google's Customer
Reliability Engineering team.

00:37:16.880 --> 00:37:17.780
Nick is here.

00:37:17.780 --> 00:37:21.980
Thank you so much
for all your help.

00:37:21.980 --> 00:37:24.290
They came and trained
some of our teams.

00:37:24.290 --> 00:37:27.830
They also provided us with
a template of assessments

00:37:27.830 --> 00:37:30.789
that we work with teams
on and say, like, do you

00:37:30.789 --> 00:37:32.330
have some of the
things that you need

00:37:32.330 --> 00:37:34.700
to think about
when you are trying

00:37:34.700 --> 00:37:38.420
to build more reliability
into your applications,

00:37:38.420 --> 00:37:40.550
and allows us to have
the kind of conversations

00:37:40.550 --> 00:37:45.180
that the CRE team with us
very early on in the process.

00:37:45.180 --> 00:37:47.930
And then, of course, as
a part of the assessment,

00:37:47.930 --> 00:37:50.570
you can score high or
low on certain aspects

00:37:50.570 --> 00:37:53.480
of your reliability matrix.

00:37:53.480 --> 00:37:56.780
And we make sure that
the work that you

00:37:56.780 --> 00:37:59.360
need to do to build
more reliability

00:37:59.360 --> 00:38:02.630
on your applications are
tracked on an ongoing basis,

00:38:02.630 --> 00:38:04.610
so that it doesn't get lost.

00:38:04.610 --> 00:38:07.400
This is all work that comes
together, and when it comes

00:38:07.400 --> 00:38:10.650
together it's really nice.

00:38:10.650 --> 00:38:13.110
And I mentioned being on call.

00:38:13.110 --> 00:38:16.570
This, to us, was probably
the biggest culture shift.

00:38:16.570 --> 00:38:22.440
So on call freaks
people out sometimes.

00:38:22.440 --> 00:38:23.930
Like, it used to freak me out.

00:38:23.930 --> 00:38:27.240
It doesn't anymore because we
have built a lot of maturity

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:28.620
around on-call.

00:38:28.620 --> 00:38:33.150
But the initial nervousness
is very hard to overcome.

00:38:33.150 --> 00:38:35.540
And we had to go
have conversations

00:38:35.540 --> 00:38:38.330
with a lot of teams and a lot
of engineers, who are very, very

00:38:38.330 --> 00:38:40.090
nervous about what
happens if I get called

00:38:40.090 --> 00:38:41.548
in the middle of
night, and I don't

00:38:41.548 --> 00:38:42.840
know how to fix the problem?

00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:48.650
So the idea is to build more
confidence in your processes

00:38:48.650 --> 00:38:50.990
so that your engineers,
your developers,

00:38:50.990 --> 00:38:53.930
can feel more confident
when being on call.

00:38:53.930 --> 00:38:59.240
We asked teams to develop
playbooks, run books, solutions

00:38:59.240 --> 00:39:02.570
to common problems that could
be shared across the board,

00:39:02.570 --> 00:39:08.330
perform war games like which is
site [? outted ?] simulations

00:39:08.330 --> 00:39:10.940
that people can work
through and actually

00:39:10.940 --> 00:39:12.920
build more confidence
in their abilities

00:39:12.920 --> 00:39:15.890
to support an application
when they are actually

00:39:15.890 --> 00:39:17.900
going on call.

00:39:17.900 --> 00:39:21.700
And it continues to be an
ongoing conversation with teams

00:39:21.700 --> 00:39:27.090
to build more and more
operational maturity.

00:39:27.090 --> 00:39:30.250
So some of the lessons learned
through this whole process,

00:39:30.250 --> 00:39:32.690
as Prashanth
mentioned, was do not

00:39:32.690 --> 00:39:36.740
try to replicate your data
center setups in the cloud.

00:39:36.740 --> 00:39:39.290
Sometimes it'll work--
sometimes it won't.

00:39:39.290 --> 00:39:46.161
Be very, very aware of what it
means to run in a public cloud

00:39:46.161 --> 00:39:48.410
and what are some of the
constraints it comes with it.

00:39:48.410 --> 00:39:50.810
Or what are some of the
constraints of your data center

00:39:50.810 --> 00:39:52.895
that may not be constraints
in the public cloud?

00:39:52.895 --> 00:39:56.150
Our networking
journey was actually

00:39:56.150 --> 00:40:02.240
a very, very good learning
example for a lot of people.

00:40:02.240 --> 00:40:06.189
We tried our best to work
against the fabric of Google,

00:40:06.189 --> 00:40:07.230
and that didn't work out.

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:09.530
It was just not the
way Google wanted us

00:40:09.530 --> 00:40:12.080
to build apps, which is fine.

00:40:12.080 --> 00:40:14.146
Google is not--

00:40:14.146 --> 00:40:15.770
I personally would
tell my team-- like,

00:40:15.770 --> 00:40:17.870
Google is not a vendor for us.

00:40:17.870 --> 00:40:18.777
They are a partner.

00:40:18.777 --> 00:40:19.610
They have done this.

00:40:19.610 --> 00:40:24.410
They've actually figured out how
to run applications, services,

00:40:24.410 --> 00:40:26.700
and sites at scale.

00:40:26.700 --> 00:40:28.640
This is an opportunity
for not just

00:40:28.640 --> 00:40:31.440
them to learn from us but us
to learn from Google as well.

00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:33.890
So treat them as a
partner, figure out

00:40:33.890 --> 00:40:37.280
what are the best practices
for your cloud provider--

00:40:37.280 --> 00:40:38.730
in this case, Google--

00:40:38.730 --> 00:40:42.440
to actually run your
applications there.

00:40:42.440 --> 00:40:45.710
Review your application
architecture,

00:40:45.710 --> 00:40:47.480
connectivity, dependencies.

00:40:47.480 --> 00:40:51.440
Like, there were times when we
had an app running in Google

00:40:51.440 --> 00:40:56.367
as well as our data center with
certain downstream apps using

00:40:56.367 --> 00:40:58.700
the Google side of things,
certain downstream apps using

00:40:58.700 --> 00:41:02.600
the data center
side, or an app that

00:41:02.600 --> 00:41:05.360
didn't have enough
confidence in their GCP setup

00:41:05.360 --> 00:41:07.370
still having the data
center as a fallback

00:41:07.370 --> 00:41:09.180
and then gradually
increasing the traffic

00:41:09.180 --> 00:41:11.240
as they built more
maturity in the Cloud.

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:15.700
So continue to load test.

00:41:15.700 --> 00:41:18.530
The assumptions you make when
you are running in your data

00:41:18.530 --> 00:41:21.227
center may not hold true when
you're running in the cloud.

00:41:21.227 --> 00:41:22.310
You may need more compute.

00:41:22.310 --> 00:41:24.160
You may need less compute.

00:41:24.160 --> 00:41:27.900
You may need more memory, like
things like that are important,

00:41:27.900 --> 00:41:30.410
so continue to benchmark your
applications during the course

00:41:30.410 --> 00:41:33.311
of the migration.

00:41:33.311 --> 00:41:35.060
In general, it's a
great thing to continue

00:41:35.060 --> 00:41:36.680
to benchmark your
apps through course

00:41:36.680 --> 00:41:40.527
of [INAUDIBLE] and like
deployments, et cetera.

00:41:40.527 --> 00:41:42.110
One of the things
that we did early on

00:41:42.110 --> 00:41:46.580
was manage a centralized
launch calendar.

00:41:46.580 --> 00:41:49.190
So teams are aware
of their dependencies

00:41:49.190 --> 00:41:53.450
and were always aware of
when their dependencies would

00:41:53.450 --> 00:41:56.390
be moving to the cloud or
when their dependencies would

00:41:56.390 --> 00:41:58.610
be ready in the cloud.

00:41:58.610 --> 00:42:01.070
So they could
actually coordinate

00:42:01.070 --> 00:42:06.640
with their dependencies or
like the depending systems

00:42:06.640 --> 00:42:10.500
to be able to talk to
their dependent systems.

00:42:10.500 --> 00:42:12.380
Again, I talked about
the CRE engagement.

00:42:12.380 --> 00:42:15.080
Like, it was invaluable
for us to have access

00:42:15.080 --> 00:42:16.880
to Nick and his team,
and it continues

00:42:16.880 --> 00:42:19.550
to be invaluable for us.

00:42:19.550 --> 00:42:22.670
It's been amazing for us
to interact with them,

00:42:22.670 --> 00:42:26.000
learn from them, and bring their
practices back to the teams

00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:28.950
that we work with.

00:42:28.950 --> 00:42:32.450
In terms of what's next,
we want to continue

00:42:32.450 --> 00:42:34.880
educating teams,
educating ourselves

00:42:34.880 --> 00:42:36.710
in SRE best practices.

00:42:36.710 --> 00:42:41.010
We want to employ
those across the board.

00:42:41.010 --> 00:42:43.640
So for that, we want to build
tools, instrument metrics

00:42:43.640 --> 00:42:46.910
in a common way, so
that we're always

00:42:46.910 --> 00:42:49.440
talking the same language.

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:51.110
We want to build
organization-wide SLO

00:42:51.110 --> 00:42:54.750
dashboards so that we are
always aware of what's going on.

00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:59.440
Like, observability is important
to us, especially a news

00:42:59.440 --> 00:43:05.210
site that relies on
very high uptime.

00:43:05.210 --> 00:43:07.940
News cycles are spiky.

00:43:07.940 --> 00:43:09.134
News can happen at any time.

00:43:09.134 --> 00:43:11.300
We want to make sure that
we are building a lot more

00:43:11.300 --> 00:43:13.290
reliability into
our applications,

00:43:13.290 --> 00:43:17.000
so we can provide the most
benefit to our readers.

00:43:17.000 --> 00:43:21.290
We want to-- I mentioned test
automation very early on.

00:43:21.290 --> 00:43:23.000
It's a fairly new
practice that we

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:24.380
are adopting, but at the same--

00:43:24.380 --> 00:43:26.960
what we want to do is
make sure that automation

00:43:26.960 --> 00:43:31.310
is a first-class citizen in all
CI/CD pipelines that we built,

00:43:31.310 --> 00:43:35.840
so provide tooling for us
to embed functional tasks,

00:43:35.840 --> 00:43:39.630
end-to-end tests, load tests
within our CI/CD pipelines

00:43:39.630 --> 00:43:47.150
so we can run them as a part of
every deployment or on demand.

00:43:47.150 --> 00:43:51.290
And while we do this, we want
to celebrate our successes.

00:43:51.290 --> 00:43:53.720
So that's one of the
things we're doing here,

00:43:53.720 --> 00:43:57.020
is actually talking about
some of our successes.

00:43:57.020 --> 00:43:58.430
We've learned a lot from it.

00:43:58.430 --> 00:44:02.280
We want everyone else to
learn from it as well.

00:44:02.280 --> 00:44:05.210
And if any of this sounds
interesting to you,

00:44:05.210 --> 00:44:06.410
please come join us.

00:44:06.410 --> 00:44:07.490
We are hiring.

00:44:07.490 --> 00:44:11.580
We want to work with
smart people, people

00:44:11.580 --> 00:44:15.950
who we can learn from, so
it's always interesting.

00:44:15.950 --> 00:44:20.900
We always talk about our
experiences on the open blog

00:44:20.900 --> 00:44:26.580
that we publish, so yeah,
that's pretty much it.

00:44:29.390 --> 00:44:31.210
OK then, thank you, everyone.

00:44:31.210 --> 00:44:32.980
And we are here if you
want to talk to us.

00:44:32.980 --> 00:44:35.504
Like, come and talk to us.

00:44:35.504 --> 00:44:37.420
Those are the people
from the "New York Times"

00:44:37.420 --> 00:44:38.745
over here, over there.

00:44:38.745 --> 00:44:40.310
Please come talk to all of us.

00:44:40.310 --> 00:44:40.990
Thank you.

00:44:40.990 --> 00:44:46.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

